00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.780
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Looking
for ideas about how

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:04.510
to scale your machine learning,
training, and prediction?

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.400
Well, you've come
to the right place.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.890
With over 500
sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.360
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.720
So if you didn't see
ML in Production--

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.000
Architecting for
Scale, then stay tuned

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.295
because here's the rewind.

00:00:18.295 --> 00:00:21.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.320
When it comes to
machine learning,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.280
there's toy ML
and production ML.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.100
Both are very important, though
different types of problems

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:33.985
to solve for.

00:00:33.985 --> 00:00:36.760
And when we say scale,
as it pertains to machine

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.390
learning it not only means
the usual "you require

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:42.820
additional servers"
but also being

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.240
able to scale up to more
training data or bigger models

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.240
and not having reduced
availability and response time

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.070
when prediction load increases.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.650
On the training side of
things, you should definitely

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:55.950
be using the tf.data API.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.010
And if you're using it
in a distributed system,

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.710
there are a couple
of handy arguments

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.510
to add to some of
your method calls

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.120
that will dramatically improve
the speed with which you

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.260
can read in data.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.510
And this becomes
especially important

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:09.760
when it comes time
to use accelerators

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.950
like GPUs and TPUs.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.630
One additional area
that may be worth

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.330
exploring if you want to have
just a little more control

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.880
of your infrastructure
is Kubeflow

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.670
which helps you run
TensorFlow on Kubernetes,

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.580
though it's not limited
to just TensorFlow.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.370
In this library, it tries to
address some of the challenges

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.850
around composability,
portability, and scalability

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:34.300
that are faced when
building larger ML systems.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.072
Turning now to the
production side of things,

00:01:37.072 --> 00:01:38.530
there are a number
of optimizations

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:39.940
that are worth exploring.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.735
The Graph Transform
Tool and TF Lite

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:44.860
allow you to optimize your
model for serving speed,

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.230
and there is even
a guide for how

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.290
to properly optimize the
batching of your prediction

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:50.860
calls.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.300
So that's handy.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.780
And finally, check out
TensorRT, a library

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.000
that allows you to take full
advantage of the Tensor Cores

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.810
in NVidia's V100 GPU.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.420
This library was developed in
conjunction with the TensorFlow

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.050
team here at Google.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.130
So when it comes to scaling
up machine learning,

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:12.310
there is a lot of options to
navigate and choices to weigh.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.770
So be sure to check
out the full talk

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.350
if you've got some
ML scaling challenges

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.240
that you are working on.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.850
Thanks for watching
this Cloud Rewind.

